    card title "Richer, fewer"

Can economics explain marriage and children? Love and babies look like the last place for prices.
    draw axes

    pause 0.3

    quiz "Incomes rise. What happens to the number of children?" judge false voice b
        intro: Here's a test, then. As people get richer they buy more of nearly everything: food, space, travel.
        right: Let's look at the countries.
        * More
        * Fewer
        * No link

Each dot is a country in 2023: income per person across, children per woman up.
    draw points

B: Niger: about six children per woman, at under two thousand dollars a head.
    draw point_1

The United States and Norway: about one and a half, at seventy-four and ninety-four thousand.
    draw point_20 point_21

And South Korea: zero point seven two, the lowest here.
    draw point_17

B: So richer means fewer. Are children an inferior good, like the cheapest noodles?
    draw inferior

Gary  (@erase inferior@)Becker sa (@draw becker@)id no in 1960, and in 1981 built a whole Treatise o (@draw treatise@)n the Family on that answer,

rational choice carried into the home.
    highlight treatise

    erase becker treatise axes points point_1 point_17 point_20 point_21

His first step: a household is a small factory. It turns bo (@draw f_cart@)ught goods and its m (@draw f_plus f_time@)embers' time into m (@draw f_arrow f_meal@)eals, a clean (@draw f_home@) home, and ch (@draw f_baby@)ildren.

B: Take a Harbourtown couple, with illustrative (@draw h0 h1 h2 n0@) numbers. In a day Nora can earn two hundred and forty doll (@draw n1@)ars at the boatyard, or make six (@draw n2@) home meals.

Erik earns a hundred and twenty, or makes the same six meals.
    draw e0 e1 e2

So a meal costs Nora forty dollars of pay, and Erik only twenty.
    draw cost_box cost_line_1

    erase cost

B: Comparative advantage, the logic that makes trade between strangers pay. Erik cooks, Nora works:
    highlight e2 n1 effect circle

B: two hundred and forty dollars and six meals, against a hun (@draw split@)dred and eighty if each split the day.
    draw together

That's Becker's reason to marry: a firm of two gains from dividing the work.
    draw gain

    erase f_cart f_plus f_time f_arrow f_meal f_home f_baby

And in 19 (@draw y1981@)81 it was usually the husband who earned more, so the model s (@draw home@)ent the wife home.

    pause 0.5

## The price of a child
use: indifference_budget
with:
    income: 4000
    price_x: 400
    price_y: 1
    preference: y_leaning
    curves: 1
    x_good: Children
    y_good: Other ($k)
    x_max: 14
    y_max: 5500
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 50
      w: 660
      h: 620
sources:
    - id: becker1981
      title: A Treatise on the Family
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1981
      finding: The price of children includes the parents' time, mostly the mother's; higher earning power for women raises the price of children and lowers fertility.
    - id: beckerlewis1973
      title: On the Interaction between the Quantity and Quality of Children
      authors: Gary S. Becker; H. Gregg Lewis
      year: 1973
      finding: Spending on children depends on number × quality, so the shadow price of an extra child rises with quality per child, and the price of quality rises with the number of children.
    - id: bds2005
      title: The More the Merrier? The Effect of Family Size and Birth Order on Children's Education
      authors: Sandra E. Black; Paul J. Devereux; Kjell G. Salvanes
      year: 2005
      finding: 'Norwegian population data: family size is negatively correlated with children''s education, but the effect becomes negligible once birth order is included; twin births as an instrument support this.'

    scratch price cites ["becker1981"] work ["a child: $400k","½ money","½ her lost pay"] x 845 y 470 font_size 26 hidden true
    text kids "{consumer.x:1} children" #b5482e x 845 y 560 font_size 44 hidden true
    scratch why work ["her pay × 2:","income +25%","child price +50%"] x 845 y 400 font_size 26 hidden true
    scratch nq cites ["beckerlewis1973"] work ["n = how many children","quality = how much in each","spending = n × quality × price"] x 500 y 420 font_size 32 hidden true
    text norway "Norway: size barely matters" cites ["bds2005"] x 500 y 250 font_size 32 hidden true
    text stamp2 "quantity–quality: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 590 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work ["one more child: + quality × price","a bit more quality: + n × price","quality up → n dearer, and back"] x 500 y 420 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "The price of a child"

Specialising explains who does which work. The same price of time explains how many children a couple has.
    draw axes

B: A child costs money,  (@draw price_box price_line_1@)sure. But mostly it costs hours, and in Becker's day those (@draw price_line_2 price_line_3@) were mostly the mother's.

Draw one family, illustrative numbers in thous (@draw budget_line@)ands of dollars over a lifetime: children across, everything else up.

B: Say they have four million, counting w (@highlight budget_line@)hat she could earn. Each child costs four hundred thousand: half money, half her lost pay.

    erase price

Thei (@draw ic_1@)r best mix touches the highest ind (@draw optimum guide_x guide_y@)ifference curve they can r (@draw kids@)each: three children.

B: Now women's pay doubles. They're richer. Shouldn't they have more children?
    highlight kids

Richer, yes: five million. But each child now costs six hundred thousand, because every hour at home costs her twice as much.
    animate income 5000 price_x 600 duration 3.5 ghost true

The line swings: more of everything else, and fewer children. Two and a half.
    highlight kids effect circle

B: So the subs (@draw why_box why_line_1 why_line_2@)titution effect beats the income effect. Children got (@draw why_line_3@) dearer faster than the family got richer.

    erase why

Try it: drag the children end of the budget line in and out, and watch how many children the family chooses.
    explore

    erase kids

B: There's a s (@fade axes budget_line budget_line_ghost ic_1 ic_1_ghost optimum optimum_ghost guide_x guide_y guide_x_ghost guide_y_ghost to 0.15@)econd twist: quality. Parents also choose how (@draw nq_box nq_line_1 nq_line_2@) much to put into each child: schooling, lessons, time.

Becker and Gregg Lewis showed in 1973 tha (@draw nq_line_3@)t the two feed each other. The more you put into each child,  (@highlight nq_line_3 effect underline@)the dearer one more child becomes.

B: Does it hold? In Norway, Black, Devereux and Salvanes found t (@draw norway@)hat once birth order is counted, family size barely changes how much  (@highlight nq_line_1@)schooling children get.

So the price of a woman's time fits the fall in fertility well. The trade between how  (@draw stamp2@)many and how much is still contested.

    hide norway nq stamp2

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: The interaction is one line of algebra.

@math

    erase mathq

Spending is the number of children times qu (@draw m_box m_line_1@)ality times price. Add one child, and you add quality times pric (@highlight m_line_1@)e: dearer when each child gets more.

B: Add a bit of quality (@draw m_line_2@), and you pay it for every chil (@highlight m_line_2@)d: n times price.

So when one rises, the other  (@draw m_line_3@)gets dearer. That's why the two tend to move (@highlight m_line_1 m_line_2@) in opposite directions.

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    show norway nq stamp2

    pause 0.5

## Whose choice?
sources:
    - id: becker1981
      title: A Treatise on the Family
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1981
      finding: Models the household with a single set of preferences (an altruistic head), so income is pooled.
    - id: manserbrown1980
      title: 'Marriage and Household Decision-Making: A Bargaining Analysis'
      authors: Marilyn Manser; Murray Brown
      year: 1980
      finding: Models the couple as two people bargaining (Nash), with outside options.
    - id: chiappori1992
      title: Collective Labor Supply and Welfare
      authors: Pierre-André Chiappori
      year: 1992
      finding: 'The collective model: household decisions are Pareto-efficient between members with their own preferences, not those of a single unitary agent.'
    - id: lpw1997
      title: Do Husbands and Wives Pool Their Resources? Evidence from the United Kingdom Child Benefit
      authors: Shelly J. Lundberg; Robert A. Pollak; Terence J. Wales
      year: 1997
      finding: A late-1970s UK policy change transferred a substantial child allowance to wives; Family Expenditure Survey data show a shift toward women's and children's clothing relative to men's clothing, against income pooling.
    - id: bergmann1995
      title: 'Becker''s Theory of the Family: Preposterous Conclusions'
      authors: Barbara R. Bergmann
      year: 1995
      finding: Some conclusions of Becker's theory of the family are 'on their face, preposterous'; such theorizing presents family institutions as benign and intervention as useless.
    - id: bkp2015
      title: Gender Identity and Relative Income within Households
      authors: Marianne Bertrand; Emir Kamenica; Jessica Pan
      year: 2015
      finding: In US couples the distribution of the wife's share of household income drops sharply just above one-half; attributed to gender identity norms.
    - id: ahnmira2002
      title: A note on the changing relationship between fertility and female employment rates in developed countries
      authors: Namkee Ahn; Pedro Mira
      year: 2002
      finding: Across OECD countries the correlation between fertility and female participation was negative into the early 1980s and positive from the late 1980s.

