    icon wallet size 90 set twemoji x 450 y 370 or ["money bag"] icon_look picture of "dollar banknote" hidden true
    arrow benefit #c9962e from.anchor left from.ref wallet to.anchor right to.ref purse hidden true
    text benefit_lbl "child benefit, late 1970s" #c9962e cites ["lpw1997"] x 300 y 310 font_size 28 hidden true
    text c1 "women's clothes ↑" #b5482e cites ["lpw1997"] x 760 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c2 "children's clothes ↑" #b5482e cites ["lpw1997"] x 760 y 450 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c3 "relative to men's" #8f887c x 760 y 380 font_size 28 hidden true
    text berg "Bergmann 1995:\n“preposterous”" cites ["bergmann1995"] x 760 y 500 font_size 34 hidden true
    text norm "wife out-earns him?\nrare just past ½" #8a5fa8 cites ["bkp2015"] x 760 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    text s1 "money not pooled:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["lpw1997"] x 760 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s2 "time price, fewer children:\nmostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 760 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s3 "rich countries today:\ncontested" #b5482e cites ["ahnmira2002"] x 760 y 320 font_size 28 hidden true
    text berg2 "harmony assumed?\nwage gap taken as given?" #8f887c x 760 y 380 font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Whose choice?"

So one model explains a marriage and a family size.
    draw family

B: But whose choice is it?
    draw onebudget

Becker's household has one set of tastes and one budget: a unitary family. Who brings the money home makes no difference.
    highlight onebudget

B: From 1980, economists such as Marilyn Manser and  (@erase family@)Murray Brown, an (@draw her him@)d later Pierre-André Chiappori, d (@fade onebudget to 0.4@)rew two people instead, who bargain, each (@draw bargain@) with something to lose.

Then who holds the money m (@draw wallet purse@)atters. In the late 1970s Britain gave a natural experiment:

a big child allowance moved from a tax break, mostly in fathers' pay, to cash paid to mothers.
    draw benefit benefit_lbl

B: Shelly Lundberg, Robert Pollak and Terence Wales looked at what families bought. Spending (@draw c1@) on women's and childre (@draw c2@)n's clothing rose, rel (@draw c3@)ative to men's.
    highlight benefit

From wallet to purse, and the spending moved.  (@fade onebudget to 1@)One family, one budget (@draw strike@), doesn't survive that.

    erase c1 c2 c3

B: Feminist economists went further. Barbara Ber (@draw berg@)gmann called some of Becker's conclusions preposterous: the family as harmony, and (@draw berg2@) the wage gap that drives specialising taken as given.

    erase berg berg2

And norms, not prices, show up in the data. In (@draw norm@) American couples, the share of wives who earn a bit more than their  (@highlight her@)husbands drops sharply just past half.

    erase norm

How sure are we? That mon (@draw s1@)ey is not simply pooled: mostly agreed. That the price of time helpe (@draw s2@)d cut fertility: mostly agreed.

B: But across rich countries since the la (@draw s3@)te eighties, where more women work there are more children, not fewer (@highlight s3@). Why is contested.

    pause 0.3

    quiz "A microcredit programme lends to wives instead of husbands; the household's total income is the same either way. What does the unitary model predict for what the household buys?" voice b
        intro: Three cases to try the ideas on.
        right: Nothing changes: with one budget only the total counts. The bargaining evidence says otherwise.
        wrong: One family, one budget: only the total counts. Here, nothing changes.
        * More for her and the children
        + Nothing changes
        * Less for the children

    quiz "Men's wages rise; women's stay put. In Becker's model, what happens to the number of children?"
        right: More children: the family is richer, and a child's price, mostly her time, has not changed.
        wrong: Richer, same price of a child: here, more children.
        + More children
        * Fewer children
        * No change

    quiz "Two partners earn the same and are equally good at home. Comparative advantage gives them nothing, yet Becker still predicts they specialise. Why?" voice b
        right: Practice makes each better at their half: skills grow with use, so even identical partners gain by specialising over time.
        wrong: Skills built at work or at home grow with use: here, practice makes each better at their half.
        + Practice makes each better at their half
        * The wife earns less
        * Specialising never pays them

So can economics explain marriage and children? It explains a surprisin (@highlight her him@)g amount: who specialises, and why richer families have fewer children.

But a family is two people barga (@highlight strike onebudget@)ining inside norms, not one firm with one purse.

    pause 0.5

